\title{The Mathematician's Approach to Proposition and Proof}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2025-09-05}
\meta{toc}{true}

% how does the mathematician know that their definition or thing is .. correct?
% feedback loop with theory 
% Mathematicians craft propositions and proofs 


\p{
    Professional mathematicians spend their time proving theorems.
    Many people imagine professional mathematicians as doing enormous calculations, using esoteric symbols, on a chalkboard to derive equations.
    However, the reality for many (if not most) professional mathematicians is that day to day work consists of proving theorems.
    A proof of a theorem, fundamentally, is an \em{argument}, written in prose, that justifies the truth or falsehood of a proposition by logical means.
    % Equally importantly, they spend their time defining (or discovering) what needs to be proved by articulating new definitions and stating conjectures.
}

\p{
    A lot of work, skill, and creativity goes into finding a proof for a theorem.
    Conjectures can go unsolved for hundreds of years, requiring huge feats of intelligence to solve.
    However, this is not the full story.
    Most people overlook, and are surprised by, the fact that articulating the \em{correct} theorem statement, or conjecture, that one sets as a goal to prove is just as difficult, if not more, than finding a proof of that theorem.
    In my (limited) experience, it is much harder.
}

\p{
    A mathematician will start with an intuitive idea, for which there may not yet be appropriate or adequate technical language or symbols to describe it.
    He will go through a cycle of experimenting with a definition, or a conjecture, that attempts to capture the idea he's after.
    He will then test out the \em{consequences} of this definition or conjecture by either proving theorems with it, trying to prove the theorem itself, or looking at its consequences in the mathematical theory more broadly.
}

\p{
    This will kick off a process which is broadly intuitive, though crucially informed by feedback with the relevant mathematical theory, of aiming at a definition or conjecture, testing its fit, and adjusting it.
    It can take the mathematical community as a whole several iterations to actually get the process right.
    As a particularly interesting example, consider the history of the definition of [\em{compactness}](dhsorens-001I) in mathematics.
}

\p{
    Most fundamentally, a mathematician's job is to bring in novel and useful abstract concepts, capture them in their appropriate definitions or conjectures, and place them appropriately in the corpus of mathematical theory.
    Philosophers do this, often by inventing new technical terminology;
    scientists do this by defining new methods and measurements;
    artists do this but instead of technical terms and symbols, they use an artistic medium.
}

